Description: Build exr at opt-level 1, for reproducibility on arm64
 zola reproduces on amd64 and fails about half the time on arm64. The failure
 is the same LLVM non-determinism that
 deoptimise-imageproc-for-reproducibility.patch works around, surfacing in a
 different crate, so that patch does not cover it: it is architecture
 independent and amd64 was clean under it nine times out of nine while arm64
 was not.
 .
 The function was identified by building one record three times with symbols
 kept and diffing the results against each other. Two builds agreed and the
 third differed by twenty-four bytes: twenty in .note.gnu.build-id, which is a
 SHA1 over the content and therefore a consequence, and four in .text, all
 inside
 exr::compression::dwa::lossy_dct::decode_lossy_channels.
 Those four bytes are one byte each within four fixed-width AArch64
 instructions, a swap of x10 and x11 across two add/stp pairs with identical
 semantics. Register allocation, as on amd64.
 .
 That byte signature is the same one the published binary shows against a
 rebuild of itself, twenty in the build-id and four in .text, which is what
 ties the diagnostic configuration's answer to the shipped one.
 .
 The override targets exr directly, and this is the opposite of the imageproc
 case. There the unstable code was a monomorphization of fast_image_resize's
 generics instantiated in the caller, so deoptimising the defining crate would
 have changed nothing. Here the mangled name carries exr's own crate hash with
 no generic parameters from elsewhere: it is exr's own code in exr's own
 codegen unit, and exr is where the override belongs.
 .
 exr arrives through image's default features and decodes OpenEXR. A static
 site generator has no use for it, so disabling that feature would delete the
 code rather than deoptimise it. That is deliberately not done here: zola's
 manifest sets default-features = true on image, and dropping a format from a
 packaged binary is a divergence from what upstream ships, which is a larger
 decision than a reproducibility workaround.
 .
 This is a workaround, not a repair. The LLVM bug is untouched. Drop it when
 LLVM fixes the underlying non-determinism, and confirm with several builds
 before doing so -- on a leg that flaps about half the time, two agreeing
 builds happen a quarter of the time by luck.
Author: pkg.haus archive <archive@pkg.haus>
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-09-22
--- a/Cargo.toml
+++ b/Cargo.toml
@@ -4,6 +4,12 @@
 [profile.release.package.imageproc]
 opt-level = 1
 
+# See debian/patches/deoptimise-exr-for-reproducibility.patch. Same LLVM
+# non-determinism, a different crate: exr's own DWA decoder, reached only
+# through image's default features.
+[profile.release.package.exr]
+opt-level = 1
+
 [package]
 name = "zola"
 version.workspace = true
